const bdaPythonRPremiumHardQs=[{"q":"What is the output of the following Python code?\n\n```\ndef func(items=[]):    items.append(len(items))    return itemsprint(func())print(func())\n```","o":["`[0]` followed by `[0]`","`[0]` followed by `[0, 1]`","`[1]` followed by `[1, 2]`","Error"],"a":1,"e":"Default mutable arguments are created once when the function is defined and reused across calls."},{"q":"What is the output of this R code?\n\n```\nx <- matrix(1:12, nrow=3)\napply(x, 2, sum)\n```","o":["`6 15 24 33`","`22 26 30`","`1 2 3 4`","`12 11 10 9`"],"a":0,"e":"R fills matrices column-wise. `apply(x, 2, sum)` calculates the sum of each column."},{"q":"Which NumPy function returns the indices of elements that satisfy a given condition?","o":["`np.argmax()`","`np.where()`","`np.unique()`","`np.extract_indices()`"],"a":1,"e":"`np.where(condition)` returns the indices of elements where the condition is TRUE."},{"q":"What is the output of the following Python code?\n\n```\nclass A:    def show(self):        return \"A\"class B(A):    def show(self):        return super().show() + \"B\"obj = B()print(obj.show())\n```","o":["`B`","`AB`","`BA`","Error"],"a":1,"e":"`super()` invokes the parent implementation before appending `\"B\"`."},{"q":"Which R function is most suitable for applying a function to each row of a data frame and returning a vector of results?","o":["`lapply()`","`apply()`","`mapply()`","`rapply()`"],"a":1,"e":"`apply()` can operate across rows using `MARGIN = 1`, although data frames may be coerced to matrices."},{"q":"What is the output of this Python code?\n\n```\nx = [1, 2, 3]y = xx += [4]print(y)\n```","o":["`[1, 2, 3]`","`[4]`","`[1, 2, 3, 4]`","Error"],"a":2,"e":"`x += [4]` modifies the existing list in place, so `y` observes the change."},{"q":"Which pandas operation is appropriate for calculating the average salary department-wise while preserving each original row?","o":["`groupby().mean()`","`groupby().transform(\"mean\")`","`groupby().filter(\"mean\")`","`groupby().aggregate_rows()`"],"a":1,"e":"`transform()` returns group-level results aligned with the original rows."},{"q":"What does the following R code return?\n\n```\nx <- c(4, 8, 12, 16)\nx[-2]\n```","o":["`4 12 16`","`8 12 16`","`4 8 16`","`12 16`"],"a":0,"e":"Negative indexing in R excludes the specified position."},{"q":"What is the output of the following Python code?\n\n```\ndef outer(x):    def inner(y):        return x + y    return innerf = outer(10)print(f(5))\n```","o":["5","10","15","Error"],"a":2,"e":"The inner function is a closure and retains access to `x`."},{"q":"Which NumPy function is used to calculate the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of a square matrix?","o":["`np.linalg.inv()`","`np.linalg.eig()`","`np.linalg.det()`","`np.linalg.solve()`"],"a":1,"e":"`eig()` computes eigenvalues and right eigenvectors of a square matrix."},{"q":"Which R function returns the residuals of a fitted linear regression model?","o":["`fitted()`","`coef()`","`residuals()`","`formula()`"],"a":2,"e":"`residuals()` extracts observed-minus-fitted values from a fitted model."},{"q":"What is the output of this Python code?\n\n```\nx = [1, 2, 3, 4]print(list(filter(None, [0, 1, \"\", \"A\", None, 5])))\n```","o":["`[0, 1, \"\", \"A\", None, 5]`","`[1, \"A\", 5]`","`[0, None]`","`[1, 5]`"],"a":1,"e":"`filter(None, iterable)` retains truthy elements and removes falsy values."},{"q":"In R, what is the purpose of `with()` when working with a data frame?","o":["It permanently modifies every column","It evaluates expressions in the data frame's environment","It converts all columns into factors","It removes missing values automatically"],"a":1,"e":"`with()` allows columns to be referenced by name within an expression without repeatedly using `$`."},{"q":"Which Python method is used to define custom behavior for the `len()` function on a user-defined class?","o":["`__length__()`","`__size__()`","`__len__()`","`__count__()`"],"a":2,"e":"Python calls `__len__()` when `len()` is applied to an object."},{"q":"What is the output of the following R code?\n\n```\nx <- c(2, 4, 6)\ny <- c(10, 20)\nx + y\n```","o":["`12 24 6`","`12 24 16`","`12 24 16` with a warning","Error"],"a":2,"e":"R recycles the shorter vector. Since the longer length is not a multiple of the shorter length, a warning is issued."},{"q":"Which Python construct is used to ensure that a generator can be consumed lazily rather than creating an entire list in memory?","o":["List comprehension","Generator expression","Dictionary comprehension","Tuple concatenation"],"a":1,"e":"Generator expressions yield values on demand, reducing memory consumption for large sequences."},{"q":"What does `df.loc[5]` select in pandas?","o":["The sixth row by position","The row with index label 5","The fifth column","Every row after index 5"],"a":1,"e":"`.loc` uses index labels rather than integer positions."},{"q":"Which R function is used to calculate the partial correlation between variables through a specialized package such as `ppcor`?","o":["`cor()`","`pcor()`","`cov()`","`lm()`"],"a":1,"e":"`ppcor::pcor()` calculates partial correlations while controlling for other variables."},{"q":"What is the output of this Python code?\n\n```\nx = {\"a\": 1, \"b\": 2}y = {\"b\": 5, \"c\": 7}print({**x, **y})\n```","o":["`{'a': 1, 'b': 2, 'c': 7}`","`{'a': 1, 'b': 5, 'c': 7}`","`{'b': 2, 'c': 7}`","Error"],"a":1,"e":"Dictionary unpacking merges mappings, and the later value overrides an earlier value for the same key."},{"q":"Which pandas method is designed to identify duplicate rows in a DataFrame?","o":["`isna()`","`duplicated()`","`unique()`","`dropna()`"],"a":1,"e":"`DataFrame.duplicated()` identifies rows that duplicate earlier rows according to selected columns."},{"q":"What is the output of this R code?\n\n```\nx <- list(a = 1:3, b = 4:6)\nsapply(x, mean)\n```","o":["A list containing two vectors","A numeric vector containing `2 5`","A matrix with three rows","Error"],"a":1,"e":"`sapply()` simplifies the results when possible; the means of the two vectors are 2 and 5."},{"q":"Which Python library provides `Pipeline` for combining preprocessing and estimators in machine learning?","o":["NumPy","pandas","scikit-learn","Matplotlib"],"a":2,"e":"`sklearn.pipeline.Pipeline` chains transformations and a final estimator."},{"q":"Which R function is used to extract a particular column from a data frame using its name as a string?","o":["`get()`","`[[ ]]`","`subset()` only","`attach()`"],"a":1,"e":"`df[[\"column_name\"]]` extracts a column by its character name."},{"q":"What is the output of this Python code?\n\n```\nx = [1, 2, 3]print(sum(n * n for n in x))\n```","o":["6","9","14","36"],"a":2,"e":"The generator produces 1, 4, and 9; their sum is 14."},{"q":"Which R function provides a way to fit a nonlinear least-squares model?","o":["`lm()`","`nls()`","`glm()`","`aov()`"],"a":1,"e":"`nls()` fits nonlinear models using least squares."},{"q":"What is the output of the following Python code?\n\n```\ndef add(a, b):    a.append(b)x = [1, 2]add(x, 3)print(x)\n```","o":["`[1, 2]`","`[1, 2, 3]`","`[3]`","Error"],"a":1,"e":"Lists are mutable objects. The function modifies the original list through the reference passed to it."},{"q":"Which R function is used to calculate a moving average through convolution-style filtering?","o":["`stats::filter()`","`stats::smooth()`","`stats::rolling()`","`stats::moving()`"],"a":0,"e":"`stats::filter()` applies linear filtering using specified coefficients."},{"q":"What is the output of the following Python code?\n\n```\nx = [1, 2, 3, 4]print([i for i in x if i % 2 == 0])\n```","o":["`[1, 3]`","`[2, 4]`","`[1, 2, 3, 4]`","`[4]`"],"a":1,"e":"The comprehension retains only even values."},{"q":"Which pandas function performs a pivot operation that aggregates values when duplicate index-column combinations exist?","o":["`pivot()`","`pivot_table()`","`melt()`","`explode()`"],"a":1,"e":"`pivot_table()` supports aggregation, such as mean or sum, for duplicate combinations."},{"q":"What is the output of this R code?\n\n```\nx <- c(3, 7, 2, 9, 5)\norder(x)\n```","o":["`2 3 5 1 4`","`9 7 5 3 2`","`2 3 5 7 9`","`1 2 3 4 5`"],"a":0,"e":"`order()` returns the positions that arrange the vector in ascending order."},{"q":"Which Python mechanism allows an object to be used in a `with` statement by defining entry and exit behavior?","o":["Iterator protocol","Context manager protocol","Descriptor protocol","Mapping protocol"],"a":1,"e":"The `__enter__()` and `__exit__()` methods implement the context manager protocol."},{"q":"Which R function returns the fitted coefficients of a regression model?","o":["`residuals()`","`predict()`","`coef()`","`fitted()`"],"a":2,"e":"`coef()` extracts estimated model coefficients."},{"q":"What is the output of this Python code?\n\n```\nx = [1, 2, 3]y = x[:]y[0] = 100print(x)\n```","o":["`[100, 2, 3]`","`[1, 2, 3]`","`[100]`","Error"],"a":1,"e":"Slicing creates a new outer list, so changing an element in `y` does not affect `x`."},{"q":"Which NumPy function is used to stack arrays vertically?","o":["`np.hstack()`","`np.vstack()`","`np.dstack()`","`np.column_stack()` only"],"a":1,"e":"`vstack()` stacks arrays along the first axis, adding rows when dimensions are compatible."},{"q":"Which R function is useful for fitting a model with a Poisson distribution and a log link?","o":["`lm()`","`nls()`","`glm()`","`aov()`"],"a":2,"e":"A Poisson generalized linear model can be fitted using `glm(y ~ x, family = poisson(link = \"log\"))`."},{"q":"What is the output of this Python code?\n\n```\nx = [1, 2, 3]print(list(enumerate(x, start=1)))\n```","o":["`[(0, 1), (1, 2), (2, 3)]`","`[(1, 1), (2, 2), (3, 3)]`","`[(1, 2), (2, 3), (3, 4)]`","`[1, 2, 3]`"],"a":1,"e":"The `start=1` argument makes enumeration begin at index 1."},{"q":"Which R function can be used to calculate the determinant of a square matrix?","o":["`solve()`","`eigen()`","`det()`","`diag()`"],"a":2,"e":"`det()` calculates the determinant of a matrix."},{"q":"Which pandas method performs forward filling of missing values?","o":["`fillna(method=\"bfill\")`","`fillna(method=\"ffill\")`","`dropna()`","`interpolate(method=\"nearest\")` only"],"a":1,"e":"Forward filling propagates the last valid observation into subsequent missing positions. In current pandas versions, `ffill()` is also available directly."},{"q":"What is the output of this R code?\n\n```\nx <- c(1, 2, 3, 4)\ny <- ifelse(x > 2, \"High\", \"Low\")\nlength(y)\n```","o":["2","3","4","1"],"a":2,"e":"`ifelse()` returns one result for every element of the input vector."},{"q":"Which Python method is responsible for defining how an object is represented as a string for users?","o":["`__repr__()`","`__str__()`","`__format__()` only","`__bytes__()`"],"a":1,"e":"`str(object)` calls `__str__()` to obtain its informal string representation."},{"q":"Which R function can fit a model with a binomial response and a logit link?","o":["`glm()`","`lm()`","`nls()`","`aov()`"],"a":0,"e":"Logistic regression can be fitted with `glm(..., family = binomial(link = \"logit\"))`."},{"q":"What is the output of this Python code?\n\n```\nx = [1, 2, 3]print(list(zip(x, x[::-1])))\n```","o":["`[(1, 1), (2, 2), (3, 3)]`","`[(1, 3), (2, 2), (3, 1)]`","`[(3, 1), (2, 2), (1, 3)]`","`[(1, 3), (2, 1)]`"],"a":1,"e":"The reversed list is `[3, 2, 1]`; `zip()` pairs corresponding elements."},{"q":"Which NumPy operation creates a view of an array when possible rather than necessarily copying its underlying data?","o":["`np.copy()`","Basic slicing","`np.array(x, copy=True)`","`np.load()`"],"a":1,"e":"Basic slicing generally returns a view of the original array, while advanced indexing generally returns a copy."},{"q":"Which R function calculates the variance of a numeric vector?","o":["`sd()`","`var()`","`cov()`","`mean()`"],"a":1,"e":"`var()` calculates sample variance using the denominator \\\\(n-1\\\\) by default."},{"q":"What is the output of this Python code?\n\n```\nx = {\"a\": 1, \"b\": 2}print(\"c\" in x)\n```","o":["True","False","None","Error"],"a":1,"e":"Membership testing on a dictionary checks its keys."},{"q":"Which pandas operation is most appropriate for calculating the number of unique customers in each city?","o":["`df.groupby(\"city\")[\"customer\"].nunique()`","`df[\"customer\"].unique()`","`df.groupby(\"customer\")[\"city\"].count()`","`df[\"city\"].nunique()`"],"a":0,"e":"Grouping by city and applying `nunique()` to the customer column counts distinct customers in each group."},{"q":"Which R function allows a user to define a custom function with arguments and a body?","o":["`function()`","`define()`","`method()`","`lambda()`"],"a":0,"e":"R functions are defined using the `function()` keyword."},{"q":"What is the output of this Python code?\n\n```\nx = [1, 2, 3]print(sum(map(lambda n: n ** 2, x)))\n```","o":["6","9","14","36"],"a":2,"e":"The squared values are 1, 4 and 9, whose sum is 14."},{"q":"Which R function can be used to calculate a correlation test and return a p-value?","o":["`cor()`","`cor.test()`","`cov()`","`lm()`"],"a":1,"e":"`cor.test()` tests for association and reports a test statistic, confidence interval where applicable, and p-value."},{"q":"Which Python feature allows a class to control how its attributes are accessed or assigned?","o":["Properties","List comprehensions","Generators","Iterators only"],"a":0,"e":"The `property` mechanism allows custom getter, setter and deleter behavior for attributes."},{"q":"What is the output of this Python code?\n\n```\nclass Counter:    count = 0    def __init__(self):        Counter.count += 1a = Counter()b = Counter()c = Counter()print(Counter.count)\n```","o":["0","1","2","3"],"a":3,"e":"`count` is a class variable shared by all instances. Each initialization increments it."},{"q":"Which R function is used to evaluate an expression in an environment where variables can be modified locally without permanently attaching a data frame?","o":["`attach()`","`with()`","`within()`","`environment()`"],"a":2,"e":"`within()` evaluates expressions in a modified copy of an object, returning the modified object. `with()` evaluates expressions but does not return modifications to the data frame."},{"q":"What is the output of the following Python code?\n\n```\ndef make_multiplier(n):    return lambda x: x * ndouble = make_multiplier(2)triple = make_multiplier(3)print(double(5) + triple(5))\n```","o":["15","20","25","30"],"a":1,"e":"The closures retain different values of `n`: 10 + 15 = 25."},{"q":"Which NumPy function can efficiently calculate the inverse of a square matrix?","o":["`np.linalg.inv()`","`np.linalg.det()`","`np.linalg.norm()`","`np.linalg.eigvals()`"],"a":0,"e":"`np.linalg.inv()` calculates the matrix inverse when the matrix is nonsingular."},{"q":"What does the following R expression return?\n\n```\nx <- c(10, 20, 30, 40, 50)\nx[c(1, 3, 5)] <- c(100, 300, 500)\nx\n```","o":["`10 20 30 40 50`","`100 300 500`","`100 20 300 40 500`","Error"],"a":2,"e":"R replaces the elements at positions 1, 3 and 5 with the supplied values."},{"q":"Which Python module is used to implement multiprocessing with separate processes?","o":["`threading`","`multiprocessing`","`asyncio`","`concurrent.futures.thread`"],"a":1,"e":"The multiprocessing module provides process-based parallelism using separate processes."},{"q":"Which pandas function is useful for calculating a rolling window mean over a time-ordered Series?","o":["`expanding()`","`rolling()`","`resample()`","`cumsum()`"],"a":1,"e":"`rolling()` creates a rolling window object on which operations such as `.mean()` can be applied."},{"q":"What is the output of this Python code?\n\n```\nx = [1, 2, 3]it = iter(x)print(next(it))print(next(it))\n```","o":["1 then 1","1 then 2","2 then 3","Error"],"a":1,"e":"Each call to `next()` advances the iterator to its next element."},{"q":"Which R function can be used to create a model matrix from a formula?","o":["`model.matrix()`","`matrix.model()`","`lm.matrix()`","`design.matrix()`"],"a":0,"e":"`model.matrix()` creates a design matrix from a formula, including appropriate encoding of factors."},{"q":"Which Python mechanism prevents a class attribute from being accessed or modified directly using the ordinary attribute name, through name mangling?","o":["Single underscore prefix","Double underscore prefix","`@staticmethod`","`@classmethod`"],"a":1,"e":"Names such as `__value` are name-mangled by Python; this is not strict access control."},{"q":"What is the output of the following R code?\n\n```\nx <- c(5, 10, 15, 20)\nwhich.max(x)\n```","o":["20","4","3","1"],"a":1,"e":"`which.max()` returns the index of the first maximum element."},{"q":"Which NumPy function is designed to broadcast arrays to a specified compatible shape without necessarily copying the data?","o":["`np.resize()`","`np.broadcast_to()`","`np.repeat()`","`np.tile()`"],"a":1,"e":"`broadcast_to()` creates a read-only broadcasted view when the requested shape is compatible."},{"q":"What is the output of this Python code?\n\n```\nx = [1, 2, 3, 4]print(list(filter(lambda n: n % 3, x)))\n```","o":["`[3]`","`[1, 2, 4]`","`[1, 2, 3, 4]`","`[]`"],"a":1,"e":"The expression `n % 3` is zero for 3, which is falsy. Other values are retained."},{"q":"Which R function can be used to perform a nonparametric Wilcoxon rank-sum test for two independent samples?","o":["`t.test()`","`wilcox.test()`","`var.test()`","`chisq.test()`"],"a":1,"e":"With two independent samples, `wilcox.test()` performs the Wilcoxon rank-sum test."},{"q":"Which Python library provides `joblib` utilities commonly used for caching and parallel execution in machine learning workflows?","o":["scikit-learn ecosystem","Tkinter","Flask","BeautifulSoup"],"a":0,"e":"Joblib is commonly used alongside scikit-learn for serialization, caching and parallel computation."},{"q":"What does `na.rm = TRUE` do in many R statistical functions?","o":["Replaces missing values with zero","Removes missing values from the calculation","Converts missing values into factors","Stops execution when missing values occur"],"a":1,"e":"It instructs supported functions to exclude missing values before calculating results."},{"q":"What is the output of this Python code?\n\n```\nx = [1, 2, 3]y = [10, 20, 30]print(list(map(lambda a, b: a + b, x, y)))\n```","o":["`[11, 22, 33]`","`[10, 20, 30]`","`[1, 2, 3, 10, 20, 30]`","Error"],"a":0,"e":"`map()` applies the function to corresponding elements from both iterables."},{"q":"Which R function can be used to generate a random sample with replacement?","o":["`sample(x, replace = FALSE)`","`sample(x, replace = TRUE)`","`rep(x, sample = TRUE)`","`random(x, replacement = TRUE)`"],"a":1,"e":"Setting `replace = TRUE` allows the same element to be selected more than once."},{"q":"Which pandas method is suitable for replacing values in a DataFrame based on a mapping dictionary?","o":["`replace()`","`map()` on the whole DataFrame only","`join()`","`stack()`"],"a":0,"e":"`DataFrame.replace()` can replace specified values using a dictionary or other supported mappings."},{"q":"What is the output of this Python code?\n\n```\nx = [1, 2, 3]print(all(n > 0 for n in x))\n```","o":["False","0","True","None"],"a":2,"e":"`all()` returns TRUE when every element satisfies the condition."},{"q":"Which R function is used to extract the confidence interval from a fitted statistical test object?","o":["`confint()`","`interval()`","`range()`","`summary()`"],"a":0,"e":"`confint()` computes confidence intervals for supported fitted model objects and parameters."},{"q":"Which Python feature allows an object to define its own behavior when used in a `for` loop?","o":["`__iter__()` and `__next__()`","`__init__()` and `__del__()`","`__str__()` and `__repr__()`","`__add__()` and `__sub__()`"],"a":0,"e":"The iterator protocol uses `__iter__()` to return an iterator and `__next__()` to produce subsequent elements."},{"q":"What does this R code return?\n\n```\nx <- c(1, 2, 3, 4)\nouter(x, x, \"*\")\n```","o":["A vector containing `1 4 9 16`","A 4 × 4 matrix of pairwise products","A 4 × 4 matrix of pairwise sums","An error"],"a":1,"e":"`outer()` applies a function to every pair of elements from the two vectors."},{"q":"Which Python operation is used to create a shallow copy of a dictionary?","o":["`dict.clear()`","`dict.copy()`","`dict.pop()`","`dict.update()`"],"a":1,"e":"`copy()` creates a new dictionary but does not recursively copy nested mutable objects."},{"q":"Which R function can be used to perform a Fisher's exact test on a contingency table?","o":["`chisq.test()`","`fisher.test()`","`t.test()`","`anova()`"],"a":1,"e":"Fisher's exact test evaluates association in contingency tables, particularly useful for small expected counts."},{"q":"What is the output of the following Python code?\n\n```\nx = [1, 2, 3]y = xx = x + [4]print(y)\n```","o":["`[1, 2, 3, 4]`","`[1, 2, 3]`","`[4]`","Error"],"a":1,"e":"`x + [4]` creates a new list and assigns it to `x`. The variable `y` still references the original list."},{"q":"Which R function is used to evaluate an expression while temporarily attaching a data frame's columns to the search path?","o":["`with()`","`within()`","`attach()`","`subset()`"],"a":2,"e":"`attach()` makes data-frame columns accessible by name through the search path. It should be used carefully because it can create name conflicts."},{"q":"Which Python library provides tools for distributed and parallel computing using task graphs?","o":["Dask","Pillow","Tkinter","csv"],"a":0,"e":"Dask supports parallel and distributed computation through task graphs and collections such as DataFrame and Array."},{"q":"What is the output of this R code?\n\n```\nx <- c(2, 4, 6, 8)\ny <- c(1, 2, 3, 4)\nsum(x / y)\n```","o":["10","8","4","12"],"a":0,"e":"Each division produces 2, so the sum is \\\\(2+2+2+2=8\\\\)."},{"q":"Which Python method is used to define a custom comparison behavior for the less-than operator?","o":["`__eq__()`","`__lt__()`","`__gt__()`","`__ne__()`"],"a":1,"e":"Python calls `__lt__()` when evaluating the less-than operator for user-defined objects."},{"q":"Which R function is useful for combining multiple data frames row-wise while matching columns by name?","o":["`rbind()`","`merge()`","`dplyr::bind_rows()`","`cbind()`"],"a":2,"e":"`bind_rows()` combines rows and aligns columns by name, filling absent columns with `NA`."},{"q":"What is the output of this Python code?\n\n```\nx = [1, 2, 3]print(sum(x, 10))\n```","o":["6","10","16","Error"],"a":2,"e":"The second argument is the starting value: 10 + 1 + 2 + 3 = 16."},{"q":"Which R function can be used to calculate the density estimate of a continuous numeric variable?","o":["`hist()`","`density()`","`table()`","`ecdf()`"],"a":1,"e":"`density()` estimates the probability density function using kernel density estimation."},{"q":"Which Python library is specifically designed to handle large-scale tabular data using parallel and out-of-core computation?","o":["Dask DataFrame","`csv`","`statistics`","`pprint`"],"a":0,"e":"Dask DataFrame supports parallel operations and can process datasets larger than memory."},{"q":"What is the output of this R code?\n\n```\nx <- c(1, 2, 3, 4, 5)\nquantile(x, 0.5)\n```","o":["2","2.5","3","5"],"a":2,"e":"The 50th percentile is the median of the vector."},{"q":"Which Python concept allows an object to provide a custom implementation of the indexing operator `[]`?","o":["`__getitem__()`","`__setattr__()`","`__call__()`","`__contains__()`"],"a":0,"e":"`__getitem__()` defines behavior for retrieving values through indexing."},{"q":"Which R function can perform a permutation test using a specialized package such as `coin`?","o":["`lm()`","`independence_test()`","`var.test()`","`aov()`"],"a":1,"e":"`coin::independence_test()` supports permutation-based independence testing."},{"q":"What is the output of this Python code?\n\n```\nx = [1, 2, 3, 4]print(sum(x[::2]))\n```","o":["4","6","10","5"],"a":0,"e":"`x[::2]` selects elements at indices 0 and 2, producing `[1, 3]`. Their sum is 4."},{"q":"Which R function can extract the model frame used by a fitted model?","o":["`model.frame()`","`model.matrix()`","`terms()`","`formula()`"],"a":0,"e":"`model.frame()` extracts the data frame containing the variables used by a model."},{"q":"Which Python library provides tools for numerical integration, optimization and scientific algorithms?","o":["NumPy only","SciPy","Flask","Requests"],"a":1,"e":"SciPy builds on NumPy and provides scientific computing routines, including optimization and integration."},{"q":"What is the output of the following R code?\n\n```\nx <- c(3, 1, 4, 1, 5)\nsort(x, decreasing = TRUE)\n```","o":["`1 1 3 4 5`","`5 4 3 1 1`","`3 1 4 1 5`","`5 3 4 1 1`"],"a":1,"e":"`decreasing = TRUE` sorts the vector in descending order."},{"q":"Which Python function can retrieve an attribute from an object using its name as a string?","o":["`getattr()`","`setattr()`","`hasattr()`","`delattr()`"],"a":0,"e":"`getattr(object, name)` retrieves the named attribute and can optionally return a default if it does not exist."},{"q":"Which R function is used to create an empirical cumulative distribution function?","o":["`density()`","`ecdf()`","`pnorm()`","`qnorm()`"],"a":1,"e":"`ecdf()` constructs an empirical cumulative distribution function from sample observations."},{"q":"Which pandas method can convert a column containing strings into datetime values?","o":["`pd.to_datetime()`","`pd.to_numeric()`","`pd.to_string()`","`pd.date_range()` only"],"a":0,"e":"`pd.to_datetime()` converts supported date representations into pandas datetime objects."},{"q":"What is the output of this Python code?\n\n```\nx = [1, 2, 3]print(next(iter(x)))\n```","o":["0","1","2","Error"],"a":1,"e":"`iter()` creates an iterator and `next()` retrieves its first element."},{"q":"Which R function can be used to fit a survival regression model using the `survival` package?","o":["`lm()`","`survfit()`","`coxph()`","`glm()`"],"a":2,"e":"`survival::coxph()` fits Cox proportional hazards regression models."},{"q":"Which Python library provides tools for accessing and manipulating hierarchical HDF5 files?","o":["h5py","BeautifulSoup","PyYAML","Requests"],"a":0,"e":"h5py provides a Python interface to the HDF5 file format, supporting groups and datasets."},{"q":"Which R function can be used to fit a robust linear regression model using the MASS package?","o":["`lm()`","`rlm()`","`glm()`","`nls()`"],"a":1,"e":"`MASS::rlm()` fits robust linear models that reduce sensitivity to outliers."},{"q":"Which Python feature enables a class to customize attribute lookup when a normal attribute is not found?","o":["`__getattr__()`","`__init__()`","`__repr__()`","`__del__()`"],"a":0,"e":"Python invokes `__getattr__()` when ordinary attribute lookup fails."},{"q":"Which R function can be used to calculate the Akaike Information Criterion for a fitted model?","o":["`BIC()`","`AIC()`","`RSS()`","`MSE()`"],"a":1,"e":"`AIC()` calculates the Akaike Information Criterion, which balances model fit against model complexity."}];
(function(){
  const originalStart = window.startCppPremium;
  window.startCppPremium = function(){
    if(selectedCourse && selectedCourse.code === "PGCP-BDA" && selectedModuleIndex === 1){
      const level = document.getElementById("cppPremiumLevel").value;
      premiumQs = level === "easy" ? bdaPythonRPremiumEasyQs : level === "moderate" ? bdaPythonRPremiumModerateQs : bdaPythonRPremiumHardQs;
      if(premiumQs.length !== 100){ alert("This bank is not ready."); return; }
      premiumIndex = 0; premiumAnswers = {};
      document.getElementById("premiumUnavailable").hidden = true;
      document.getElementById("premiumPracticePanel").hidden = false;
      renderPremium();
      return;
    }
    return originalStart.apply(this, arguments);
  };
  document.addEventListener("click", function(event){
    if(event.target.closest && event.target.closest(".module-open-btn")){
      setTimeout(function(){
        if(selectedCourse && selectedCourse.code === "PGCP-BDA" && selectedModuleIndex === 1){
          document.getElementById("cppPremiumControls").hidden = false;
          document.getElementById("premiumUnavailable").hidden = true;
        }
      }, 0);
    }
  });
})();
